
=== What does firing the passthrough cost, and what buys it back? (#33) ===

clock     : 320 MHz sys
sensor id : 0x82

-- bring-up --
  sensor id : 0x82  (legacy table)
  firmware  : 2023-03-03, fpga rev 32

-- warm-up, auto loops running --
  settled at luma 134

-- phase 0: baseline, nothing fired yet --
  handle at rest                135 135 135 | 135 135 135   part   0  wobble  0  DEAD

  THE HANDLE IS DEAD BEFORE ANYTHING WAS FIRED. This is the condition
  20260907-i2cpass blamed on the passthrough, and it is here with zero
  fires. Running the ladder against it instead.
  -- recovery ladder --
     rung 0: apply the auto mask a second time, no reset
  handle after rung 0           135 134 135 | 134 134 134   part   1  wobble  1  DEAD
     rung 1: 0x07 bit 1, the documented I2C reset
  handle after rung 1           134 134 134 | 134 134 134   part   0  wobble  0  DEAD
     rung 2: 0x07 bit 7, reset cache - what cam.c:455 flushes with
  handle after rung 2             4   4   4 | 153 153 154   part 149  wobble  1  ALIVE

  Recovered from cold. The phases below run on a board that needed a
  rung to get here, which is worth knowing when reading them.

  one fire at sensor 0x300a returns 0x36 through 0x48   (0x36, as 20260907-i2cpass read)
  handle after ONE fire           4   4   4 | 156 155 154   part 152  wobble  2  ALIVE

-- phase 1: read all 128 ArduChip registers, 3 times, NO fires --
  done. Capture still returns luma 153.
  handle after                    5   5   5 | 136 139 141   part 131  wobble  5  ALIVE

-- phase 2: 36 writes of 0x07 = 0x00, no bits set --
  done. Capture still returns luma 142.
  handle after                    4   4   4 | 149 151 153   part 145  wobble  4  ALIVE

-- phase 3: fire at the FIVE HIGH addresses, 3 visits (0x3000-0x3100) --
  done. Capture still returns luma 155.
  handle after                    4   5   5 | 171 172 173   part 167  wobble  2  ALIVE

-- phase 4: fire at the SEVEN LOW addresses, 3 visits (0x0000-0x001D) --
  done. Capture still returns luma 175.
  handle after                    5   5   5 | 176 176 176   part 171  wobble  0  ALIVE

-- phase 5: 20260907-i2cpass's STAGE A, whole and unshortened --
  visit 0 done
  visit 1 done
  visit 2 done
  done. Capture still returns luma 176.
  handle after                    5   5   5 | 176 176 176   part 171  wobble  0  ALIVE

=== what this run says ===

  baseline                                          ALIVE
  after ONE fire at 0x300a                          ALIVE
  phase 1  read all 128 ArduChip registers, 3 times, NO fires ALIVE
  phase 2  36 writes of 0x07 = 0x00, no bits set    ALIVE
  phase 3  fire at the FIVE HIGH addresses, 3 visits (0x3000-0x3100) ALIVE
  phase 4  fire at the SEVEN LOW addresses, 3 visits (0x0000-0x001D) ALIVE
  phase 5  20260907-i2cpass's STAGE A, whole and unshortened ALIVE

  NOTHING BREAKS THE HANDLE - not the pieces and not stage A whole.
  20260907-i2cpass's dead control surface DOES NOT REPRODUCE, so the
  reading in that directory - that firing 0x07 bit 0 costs the manual
  exposure - is wrong, and its stage B failed for some other reason
  this run has not found. What is now unblocked is stage B itself: the
  handle is alive on both sides of a fire, which is the precondition it
  needed and never had.

  RECOVERY: 0x07 bit 7, reset cache.
  It was needed FROM COLD, with nothing fired.
  That is cheap enough for m9 to do between frames.

-- stage B: does anything read through 0x48 track an exposure we set? --
  ladder before the sweep
    up      5  14  34  71 121 176
    down    5  14  34  71 121 176
    wobble 0, retrace 0 | H1 yes  H2 yes  -> HANDLE
  exposure 0x00010 -> luma 5,  0x00200 -> luma 176   (parting 171 against the ladder's wobble of 0)

  512 addresses swept from 0x3400. Only the ones that moved:

  sensor   at low exp     at high exp    verdict

  0 tracked, 0 unstable, 512 unchanged and not listed.
  ladder after the sweep
    up      5  14  34  71 120 175
    down    5  14  34  71 120 175
    wobble 0, retrace 0 | H1 yes  H2 yes  -> HANDLE

=== stage B ===

  NOTHING TRACKED, with a handle alive on both sides of the sweep. That is
  the first time this test has been able to say so: the two earlier runs
  that reported it had no handle and their verdicts are void.

  It is evidence against 0x48 and it is not conclusive - 512 addresses
  from 0x3400 is where an OV3640 keeps its exposure and it is not everywhere
  the value could be. Do not adopt 0x48; do not write it off on one boot
  either.
